# Copy to .env and fill in. `chmod 600 .env` — it is not secret today, but it
# will accumulate things that are.
#
#   cp .env.example .env && chmod 600 .env
#
# .env must never be committed. See .gitignore.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Image
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Pin to a digest once you have a build you trust:
#   IMAGE=ghcr.io/<owner>/obsidian-remote@sha256:...
IMAGE=ghcr.io/ky1ejs/homelab/obsidian-vault:latest

# Used by deploy.sh for `gh attestation verify --owner`.
GITHUB_OWNER=ky1ejs

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Identity — DECISIONS.md#identity-1002-not-1000
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# MUST match both the owner of the directories below AND the APP_UID/APP_GID
# baked into the image. These are build args, so changing them means a rebuild —
# Docker resolves $HOME from /etc/passwd, and a uid the image does not know makes
# $HOME resolve to / instead of /home/app. The symptom is nasty: `ob login` and
# `claude` both appear to succeed, then the credentials do not persist.
#
# Use a REAL QNAP account, not 1000 because it looks standard. QNAP allocates
# uids sequentially from 1000, so an unallocated 1000 today is the next account
# created tomorrow — which would then own the 0700 credential directories.
#
#   id <your-qnap-username>       # -> uid=… gid=…
#   getent passwd 1000 1001 1002  # -> who already holds the low uids
#
# Resolved 2026-08-08: kylejs = 1002:100. gid 100 is `everyone`, which every
# QNAP account is in, so the group bits grant nothing — the owner uid is doing
# all of the access control.
APP_UID=1002
APP_GID=100

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Host paths
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# QNAP volume names are not predictable — find yours with `ls -d /share/*/`.
# CACHEDEV1_DATA is the common default and was NOT correct here.
#
# This NAS uses CE_CACHEDEV4_DATA. The CE_ prefix marks an ENCRYPTED volume —
# chosen deliberately, with auto-unlock on. See ARCHITECTURE.md#trust-boundary for what
# that does and does not buy: it defends drives that leave the building, not a
# running NAS. All five paths below MUST stay on this volume; putting any of
# them on a plain volume silently drops them out of the encrypted set.

# The Obsidian vault. Created empty — `ob sync` fills it, it is not copied from
# the Mac. This is the only one of the five that should ever be exported over
# SMB/AFP.
VAULT_HOST_PATH=/share/CE_CACHEDEV4_DATA/obsidian/vault

# The git repo. Deliberately OUTSIDE the vault. Never point Hybrid Backup Sync
# at this — a live repo is files under mutation and will sync torn.
SNAPSHOT_HOST_PATH=/share/CE_CACHEDEV4_DATA/obsidian/snapshots

# Verified bundles. Hybrid Backup Sync points HERE, and only here.
BACKUP_HOST_PATH=/share/CE_CACHEDEV4_DATA/obsidian/backups

# Credentials. SPLIT ON PURPOSE — see ARCHITECTURE.md#volumes.
#
# The agent reads a corpus you did not entirely author, so a prompt injection
# can make it read its own filesystem. Splitting these means one compromise
# yields one credential, not both.
#
# Both: chmod 700, owned by APP_UID:APP_GID, NOT exported over SMB/AFP.
# Never back either up to Drive — the agent one is a live Anthropic token.

# Obsidian Sync credentials. Mounted into vault-sync only.
SYNC_HOME_HOST_PATH=/share/CE_CACHEDEV4_DATA/obsidian/home-sync

# Claude OAuth token. Mounted into vault-claude only.
AGENT_HOME_HOST_PATH=/share/CE_CACHEDEV4_DATA/obsidian/home-agent

# A SECOND Claude OAuth token, for vault-research. Not shareable with the one
# above, and not merely for tidiness: two Claude Code instances against one home
# directory corrupt ~/.claude.json — ARCHITECTURE.md#invariants — so pointing
# both at home-agent breaks both agents rather than one. It needs its own
# one-time `claude /login`, run inside vault-research.
#
# Same handling as the other two: chmod 700, owned by APP_UID:APP_GID, never
# exported over SMB, never backed up to Drive. This one is a live Anthropic
# token belonging to the agent that can reach the open web.
RESEARCH_HOME_HOST_PATH=/share/CE_CACHEDEV4_DATA/obsidian/home-research

# The research agent's working directory, and the ONLY volume it mounts besides
# its credentials. Deliberately OUTSIDE the vault, for two separate reasons.
#
# Security: this is the container with WebSearch and WebFetch. Keeping the vault
# out of it is what stops one session holding private data, untrusted content
# and a way out at once. See DECISIONS.md#a-third-surface-for-research.
#
# Safety: scratch-sweep.sh deletes folders here on a schedule, and it is the only
# thing in this repo that deletes anything. A sweeper with a bad path inside the
# vault would delete notes, and Obsidian Sync would propagate that to every
# device before you noticed.
#
# Do NOT point this inside VAULT_HOST_PATH. scratch-sweep.sh refuses to run if
# it finds a .git or .obsidian directory here, but that check is a backstop, not
# a licence.
SCRATCH_HOST_PATH=/share/CE_CACHEDEV4_DATA/obsidian/scratch

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Snapshots
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Backstop interval for HUMAN edits, in seconds. Agent runs are bracketed by
# hooks, not by this timer — see ARCHITECTURE.md#snapshots.
SNAPSHOT_INTERVAL=3600
SNAPSHOT_ENABLED=1

# Attribution. The agent split is what makes the repo an audit log:
#   git log --author="Claude Code" --since=1.week --stat
#
# Read by snapshot.sh AND by the move tool (`vault-mcp -stdio`, registered in
# vault-claude-mcp.json), so a move committed on its own carries the same author
# as the session that made it. Blank is treated as missing there rather than as
# an empty author, which git refuses outright.
AGENT_GIT_NAME=Claude Code
AGENT_GIT_EMAIL=agent@vault.local
HUMAN_GIT_NAME=Vault
HUMAN_GIT_EMAIL=human@vault.local

# 0 = attachments in git, which is what this vault runs and what DECISIONS.md
# records: 112 MB of the 119 MB vault. Markdown-only bundles would be ~7 MB.
#
# ONE MORE THING THIS DECIDES, since the agent gained move_file: an attachment
# cannot carry the frontmatter stamp a note does, so a git commit is the only
# durable record that the agent moved an image. Set this to 1 and that record
# goes too — vault-mcp's audit log becomes the sole trace, and `git log` will
# show notes being filed and nothing about the pictures that moved with them.
#
# This template shipped 1 until 2026-08-12, contradicting that decision. The
# failure it invites is quiet: copy the template onto a rebuilt NAS, and backups
# keep succeeding while silently omitting 94% of the vault by size — noticed at
# restore, when the notes come back without their images.
#
# Still the hard-to-reverse direction, and that has not changed. Git keeps every
# version of every blob permanently, so a repo that has carried attachments
# cannot be shrunk without rewriting history and re-issuing every bundle. Set
# this to 1 before the first commit if you want markdown-only; afterwards it only
# stops NEW attachments, and the old ones stay in history regardless.
# DECISIONS.md#backups-bundles-and-why-only-one.
EXCLUDE_ATTACHMENTS=0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Backups
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Standard five-field cron, UTC, run by the vault-cron service (supercronic).
# Kept here rather than in QNAP's /etc/config/crontab so the schedule is in git,
# survives reboots and firmware updates, and is visible to anyone reading this
# repo. Validated at container start — a malformed expression fails loudly
# instead of silently never firing.
#
# Point any off-NAS sync (Hybrid Backup Sync -> Drive) at least an hour LATER
# than a daily schedule. On an hourly schedule, run the sync hourly too but
# offset — e.g. '30 * * * *' here means HBS at :30 past, never mid-write.
#
# Running hourly is cheap: a run whose HEAD has not moved since the last bundle
# exits immediately without writing anything, so an idle vault costs no storage
# and no upload. Frequency therefore sets how STALE the off-site copy can be,
# not how much it costs.
BACKUP_SCHEDULE=0 * * * *

# Retention.
#
# The live backup is a SINGLE file, `vault-latest.bundle[.age]`, replaced in
# place every run. A bundle holds the complete history, so that one file already
# contains every point in time — clone it and `git checkout 'HEAD@{2026-08-01}'`.
# Keeping a pile of stamped copies is the right shape for backups that each hold
# ONE point in time; here it is near-pure duplication at 100+ MB a copy.
#
# The stamped copies below are NOT for recovering old notes. They exist so that
# a corrupted, ransomwared or history-rewritten repo cannot overwrite your only
# good bundle before you notice. Size them by how long you would take to notice,
# not by how far back you want to read.
#
# First successful bundle of each hour is kept in hourly/, first of each day in
# daily/, first of each month in monthly/. Keyed to the calendar rather than a
# clock hour that owns each slot, so it works at any schedule and never skips a
# day just because nothing had changed at 03:00.
#
# 1 + 18 + 7 + 3 = 29 files. Size follows EXCLUDE_ATTACHMENTS above:
#
#   EXCLUDE_ATTACHMENTS=0  ~112 MB a bundle -> ~3.2 GB total
#   EXCLUDE_ATTACHMENTS=1  ~7 MB a bundle   -> ~200 MB total
#
# hourly/ is sized to a waking day: 18 covers corruption introduced at any point
# from morning to night and noticed before bed, recoverable to the hour rather
# than to that morning's daily/ copy. It is not history — one bundle already
# holds every point in time — so past a day there is nothing left to buy, which
# is where daily/ takes over. DECISIONS.md#backups-bundles-and-why-only-one.
#
# This is storage, not upload. A run whose HEAD has not moved writes nothing at
# all, so idle hours cost nothing — and hourly/ therefore retains the last N
# hours that CHANGED, which on a quiet vault can reach back much further than N
# hours on the clock.
BACKUP_KEEP_HOURLY=18
BACKUP_KEEP_DAILY=7
BACKUP_KEEP_MONTHLY=3

# Empty means PLAINTEXT personal notes on Google Drive.
# ARCHITECTURE.md#trust-boundary.
#
# If you set this: generate the keypair ON THE MAC with `age-keygen`, put only
# the public recipient here, and store the private key off both the NAS and
# Drive — password manager plus a paper copy. A bundle you cannot decrypt is
# not a backup.
AGE_RECIPIENT=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Agent
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

TMUX_SESSION=vault
AGENT_MEM_LIMIT=6g

# vault-research is a second always-on Claude Code process, so it needs its own
# cap rather than sharing AGENT_MEM_LIMIT. Claude Code wants 4 GB minimum and 8
# GB recommended, and these two caps have to fit in RAM TOGETHER alongside QTS
# and the other stacks. Two 6g agents do not fit in the ~11.7 GB this NAS had
# before the memory upgrade; check free memory before raising either.
RESEARCH_MEM_LIMIT=6g

# How long a scratch topic folder survives before scratch-sweep.sh deletes it.
# Counted from the folder's mtime, so an active topic keeps renewing itself.
#
# Must be a positive integer. 0 is REFUSED rather than treated as "sweep
# everything": it would delete the folder the agent is working in right now. To
# turn sweeping off, stop the vault-research-sweep service.
SCRATCH_RETENTION_DAYS=7

# When the sweep runs. An hour after BACKUP_SCHEDULE's default so the two are
# not competing for disk. Standard five-field cron, UTC.
SCRATCH_SWEEP_SCHEDULE=0 4 * * *

# Identity recorded in vault-mcp's audit log for the research agent's tool
# calls, so a fetch from this surface is distinguishable from vault-claude's
# moves. Not a git identity: the scratch volume is not a repo.
RESEARCH_GIT_NAME=Claude Research
RESEARCH_GIT_EMAIL=research@vault.local

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# fetch_attachment
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The research agent's download tool, served by vault-mcp over stdio and enabled
# only there (MCP_FETCH=1 in vault-research-mcp.json). It exists because nothing
# else can put an image or a PDF on disk: WebFetch returns text and never a
# file, Write cannot produce binary, and Bash is denied on every surface here.
#
# The bytes never reach a model — the tool returns a filename, a size and a
# type. See vault-mcp/fetch.go for what it refuses and why.

# Largest file it will save. 25 MiB by default, which is a generous scan of a
# magazine page and well under anything that would fill the volume by accident.
FETCH_MAX_BYTES=26214400

# Seconds for the whole request.
FETCH_TIMEOUT=60

# Comma-separated hosts fetch_attachment may reach. Matching is case-insensitive
# and on a dot boundary, so `example.com` covers `cdn.example.com` but not
# `notexample.com`.
#
# EMPTY MEANS ANY PUBLIC HOST, and that is the default on purpose. A per-topic
# allowlist does not survive real research — every new subject is a new list —
# and the control actually doing the work here is what this session can READ,
# which is nothing of yours. Private, loopback, link-local and carrier-grade-NAT
# addresses are refused at connect time regardless of this setting, so an empty
# list is not a route into the home network or the tailnet.
#
# Set it if you want both. Nothing else changes if you do.
FETCH_ALLOW_HOSTS=

# The agent's tool policy and hooks (vault-claude-settings.json) ship in the
# image and are installed into <vault>/.claude/settings.json on every start, so
# the repo is the source of truth and the vault's copy is a build artifact of
# it. That is the whole update path: edit, push, deploy.
#
# Set to 0 to pin a hand-edited settings.json in the vault instead. Drift is
# then reported in the container log rather than corrected — which also means a
# deny rule added upstream will NOT reach this agent until you merge it in
# yourself. See DECISIONS.md#shipping-the-tool-policy-with-the-image.
#
# Only an exact 0 pins it. Blank, missing or misspelled all mean MANAGED, so a
# typo here overwrites the file you were trying to pin — the opposite direction
# from VAULT_STAMP above, and for the opposite reason: leaving a security policy
# stale on the strength of a misspelling is the mistake that cannot be noticed.
#
# vault-claude REFUSES TO START if no settings.json ends up in the vault, so
# pinning with 0 means the file has to actually be there.
#
# This flag governs BOTH files install-settings.sh materialises: the tool policy
# at <vault>/.claude/settings.json and the move tool's registration at
# <vault>/.mcp.json. One flag rather than two, because they are one setting split
# across two files — the policy's allow rule for mcp__vault-tools__move_file and
# the server name in .mcp.json have to agree, and pinning one while the other
# updates is the state that produces an agent told it may use a server that does
# not exist. A missing .mcp.json is a WARNING, not a refusal to start: it costs
# the agent its move tool, not its safety. See DECISIONS.md#giving-the-agent-a-move.
VAULT_SETTINGS_MANAGED=1

# The frontmatter stamp. hook-stamp.sh runs after every Write/Edit and records
# who changed the note, in the note:
#
#   agent-created: 2026-08-20T09:11:03Z   only when this session made the note
#   agent-modified: 2026-08-29T14:02:11Z  last agent write of any kind
#   agent: claude-agent                   who made that write
#
# This duplicates what the snapshot repo already records, on purpose: the repo
# lives outside the vault, so it is invisible from Obsidian on the phone, and a
# note that leaves the vault through Sync carries none of its history.
#
# VAULT_AGENT_NAME is a vault-wide identity, not a per-stack label — vault-mcp
# writes `claude-voice` into the same three properties when it serves voice, and
# reads THIS variable when the same binary serves the agent's move tool over
# stdio, so a move and the edit that follows it carry one identity rather than
# two. The registry is the shared contract in the root README.md.
# Must match [A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*;
# the hook silently declines to stamp on anything else, because the value goes
# into YAML unquoted and a name with a colon would rewrite the note's properties
# rather than one of them.
#
# The hook is wired up in vault-claude-settings.json, which ships in the image
# and is installed into <vault>/.claude/settings.json on every agent start. Both
# a change to this variable and a change to that file take effect on the next
# recreate of vault-claude — there is nothing to copy by hand.
VAULT_AGENT_NAME=claude-agent

# Only an explicit 0 turns stamping off. Blank, missing or misspelled leaves it
# ON — the same judgement as EXCLUDE_ATTACHMENTS above, and for the same reason:
# a hook cannot refuse to start, so a typo can only fail silently, and silently
# unstamped notes cannot be identified after the fact.
VAULT_STAMP=1
